## What a `type: section` instance is

An addressable subdivision of a memorial's body — each H2+ heading and its content become a `:mm/Section` entity with its own ident, parent ref, sibling chain, and body slot.  Sections enable fine-grained addressability: cross-references can target a specific section of a memorial, not just the whole memorial.  The section graph mirrors the markdown structure: nested headings produce nested sections via `:mm.section/parent`; sibling headings at the same level form a doubly-linked sibling chain via `:mm.section/next-sibling` / `:mm.section/previous-sibling` (SIOC pairwise pattern).

## When sections are created

Sections are not authored directly — they're produced by the codec's section-tree decomposition when parsing a memorial's body.  Each `## Heading` (or deeper) becomes a section entity.  The H1 (if present) is treated as the memorial title, not a section.

Sections coexist with `:mm.memory/body-raw` which preserves the entire body verbatim — sections are an ADDRESSABLE OVERLAY, not the canonical content.  On emit, the section chain reconstructs the body markdown.

